{title: As with Gladness Men of Old}
{artist: William C. Dix / Conrad Kocher}
{genre: Christmas}
{style: Carol}
{flow: Response}
{key: G}
{tempo: 92}
{time: 4/4}
{c: Chords checked against the four-part harmony of the Open Hymnal score (As_With_Gladness_Men_Of_Old-Dix)}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[G]As with [D]glad[G]ness, [C]men [D7]of [G]old
[C]Did the [G]guid[C]ing [D]star be[G]hold
[G]As with [D]joy [G]they [C]hailed [D7]its [G]light
[C]Leading [G]on[C]ward, [D]beaming [G]bright
[G]So, most glorious [D]Lord, may [G]we
[C]Ever[G]more be [D]led to [G]Thee.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[G]As with [D]joy[G]ful [C]steps [D7]they [G]sped
[C]To that [G]low[C]ly [D]manger [G]bed
[G]There to [D]bend [G]the [C]knee [D7]be[G]fore
[C]Him whom [G]Heaven [C]and [D]earth a[G]dore;
[G]So may we with [D]willing [G]feet
[C]Ever [G]seek Thy [D]mercy [G]seat.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[G]As they [D]off[G]ered [C]gifts [D7]most [G]rare
[C]At that [G]man[C]ger [D]rude and [G]bare;
[G]So may [D]we [G]with [C]ho[D7]ly [G]joy,
[C]Pure and [G]free [C]from [D]sin’s al[G]loy,
[G]All our costliest [D]treasures [G]bring,
[C]Christ, to [G]Thee, our [D]heavenly [G]king.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
[G]Holy [D]Je[G]sus, [C]e[D7]very [G]day
[C]Keep us [G]in [C]the [D]narrow [G]way;
[G]And, when [D]earth[G]ly [C]things [D7]are [G]past,
[C]Bring our [G]ran[C]somed [D]souls at [G]last
[G]Where they need no [D]star to [G]guide,
[C]Where no [G]clouds Thy [D]glory [G]hide.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 5}
[G]In the [D]hea[G]venly [C]coun[D7]try [G]bright,
[C]Need they [G]no [C]cre[D]ated [G]light;
[G]Thou its [D]light, [G]its [C]joy, [D7]its [G]crown,
[C]Thou its [G]sun [C]which [D]goes not [G]down;
[G]There forever [D]may we [G]sing
[C]Alle[G]luias [D]to our [G]king!
{end_of_verse}
